Chapter 7
What Moves a Liquidity Ratio
ما الذي يحرّك نسب السيولة
A ratio is a fraction, and a fraction moves when either half of it moves. That sounds obvious and it is the source of most of the errors candidates make in this part of the syllabus, because the direction a ratio moves depends on where it started. A transaction that raises a current ratio of 3.31 will lower a current ratio of 0.80, and the same arithmetic produces both results.
This chapter works out the rule and applies it to the transactions the exam uses. It is the one chapter in the book where you will be asked to predict a ratio rather than compute one.
Figure F507-01 is your map.
Figure F507-01. Chapter 7 roadmap: one transaction, two halves of a fraction, one rule.
