Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
7.2  Transactions that move one half only
The other family of transactions touches one half of the fraction and not the other, and those always move the ratio in the obvious direction.
Issuing shares for cash adds to current assets and nothing to current liabilities: every liquidity ratio rises and working capital rises by the full amount.
Buying equipment for cash subtracts from current assets and leaves current liabilities alone: every ratio falls. The company is not weaker in any deep sense — it swapped cash for a machine — but it is less liquid, and that is what the ratios are for.
Borrowing long-term adds cash and adds a noncurrent liability: the liquidity ratios rise. Borrowing short-term adds cash and adds a current liability, which is the equal-change case.
Writing off a bad debt reduces receivables and reduces the allowance, so the net receivable does not move at all. This one catches candidates every time: the write-off was already provided for, so nothing in any ratio changes.
Figure F507-03 collects the common ones for a company whose current ratio is above 1, which is Orontes.
Figure F507-03. Effect of common transactions, current ratio above 1.0
@table
Transaction | Current ratio | Quick ratio | Cash ratio | Working capital
Buy inventory on credit | Falls | Falls | Falls | No change
Pay a supplier in cash | Rises | Rises | Falls | No change
Collect a receivable in cash | No change | No change | Rises | No change
Sell inventory for cash at a profit | Rises | Rises | Rises | Rises
Sell inventory for cash at cost | Rises | Rises | Rises | No change
Issue shares for cash | Rises | Rises | Rises | Rises
Buy equipment for cash | Falls | Falls | Falls | Falls
Borrow long-term for cash | Rises | Rises | Rises | Rises
Declare a dividend, unpaid | Falls | Falls | Falls | Falls
Write off a bad debt, fully provided | No change | No change | No change | No change
@end
Three rows in that figure reward a second look.
Paying a supplier raises the current ratio and lowers the cash ratio. The cash ratio's numerator is cash and securities only, so it loses the whole payment while the denominator loses the same amount; starting from 1.72, which is above 1, the subtraction from both should raise it — but the numerator falls by the full $500,000 while it is smaller than the current-asset numerator, so the effect is different. Work it: 7,344,100 − 500,000 = 6,844,100 over 3,780,000 is 1.81. It rises too. The figure says "falls" for a company whose cash ratio is below 1, which is the ordinary case, and that is why the row is worth checking against your own starting point rather than memorised.
Selling inventory at a profit raises working capital; selling it at cost does not. Current assets rise by the profit in the first case and merely change form in the second.
Declaring a dividend lowers every ratio even though no money has moved, because a current liability appears. Orontes did exactly this with $400,000 at the end of 2025.
EXAM TRAP   The three that catch people
Writing off a fully provided bad debt changes nothing: the allowance already reduced the net receivable.
Declaring a dividend changes the ratios before any cash moves.
Collecting a receivable changes the cash ratio and not the current or quick ratio.
