7.3  The effect of unit sales
The syllabus names unit sales alongside the balance-sheet elements, and that is a different kind of movement. A change in volume works through the statements rather than landing on one line.
More units sold, on credit, at a profit. Inventory falls by the cost; receivables rise by the selling price. Current assets rise by the profit. The current ratio rises, the quick ratio rises more because the numerator gains the whole receivable and loses nothing, and the cash ratio does not move until the customer pays.
More units sold, for cash, at a profit. The same, but the cash ratio rises too.
Fewer units sold. Inventory accumulates. Current assets are unchanged in total if production continues — the money moves from cash into stock — so the current ratio holds while the quick ratio falls. That divergence is the early signal of the working-capital leak of Chapter 5, and it appears in the quick ratio before it appears anywhere else.
More units sold without any increase in price, when the company must buy stock to supply them. Receivables and inventory both rise, payables rise to fund the stock, and cash falls because the payment to the supplier comes before the receipt from the customer. A growing company can become less liquid precisely because it is growing, and a lender who refuses credit at that moment can end a sound business.
Figure F507-04 traces one extra sale through the five ratios.
Figure F507-04. One additional sale on credit, cost 60 and price 100
@table
Item | Movement | Effect
Inventory | − 60 | Current and quick numerators
Accounts receivable | + 100 | Current and quick numerators
Current assets | + 40 | Current ratio rises
Quick assets | + 100 | Quick ratio rises more
Cash and securities | no change | Cash ratio unchanged
Current liabilities | no change |
Working capital | + 40 | The profit on the sale
@end
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: growth that consumes liquidity
Step 1. Orontes wins a Gulf order and must hold an extra $800,000 of stock to supply it, bought on 30-day supplier terms, selling at $1,200,000 on 60-day customer terms.
Step 2. At the end of month one, the stock is bought and nothing is sold: inventory up $800,000 and payables up $800,000. Current assets 14,188,100 + 800,000 = 14,988,100 and current liabilities 4,280,000 + 800,000 = 5,080,000. The current ratio is 14,988,100 ÷ 5,080,000 = 2.95, down from 3.31.
Step 3. At the end of month two, the supplier is paid and the goods are sold on credit: cash down $800,000, inventory down $800,000, receivables up $1,200,000, payables down $800,000. Current assets are 14,988,100 − 1,600,000 = 13,388,100, plus the receivable of 1,200,000, giving $14,588,100; current liabilities are back to $4,280,000. The ratio is 14,588,100 ÷ 4,280,000 = 3.41.
Check. The ratio fell to 2.95 and then recovered above where it began, because the sale was profitable. The dip is real and it is when the company needs its credit line. A lender reading only the year-end ratio sees none of this.
