7.4  Calculating the impact
The exam asks for the impact and not just the direction, and the method is always the same three steps: restate the numerator, restate the denominator, divide.
YOUR TURN   Four transactions
Figure F507-05 lists four transactions. Orontes starts with current assets of $14,188,100, current liabilities of $4,280,000 and a current ratio of 3.31. Compute the ratio after each one, taken separately. Answers are at the end of the chapter.
Figure F507-05. Your turn: the current ratio after each transaction (complete the blanks)
@table
Transaction | New current assets | New current liabilities | New current ratio
Pay $280,000 of accrued liabilities in cash | 13,908,100 | 4,000,000 | 3.48
Buy $720,000 of inventory on credit | ________ | ________ | ________
Issue shares for $1,000,000 cash | ________ | ________ | ________
Buy equipment for $1,180,000 cash | ________ | ________ | ________
@end
Two refinements are worth knowing.
Where a transaction affects the quick ratio differently from the current ratio, the exam usually wants both. Buying inventory on credit lowers the current ratio by adding to both halves, and lowers the quick ratio much more sharply, because the numerator does not gain anything at all while the denominator grows.
Where a question gives a target, work backwards. If Orontes wanted a current ratio of exactly 4.00 by paying down payables, the amount x satisfies (14,188,100 − x) ÷ (4,280,000 − x) = 4.00. Multiplying out: 14,188,100 − x = 17,120,000 − 4x, so 3x = 2,931,900 and x = 977,300. Orontes would pay $977,300. The arithmetic is worth practising because the exam sets it regularly.
EXAM TRAP   Working backwards to a target ratio
Set up the equation with the unknown in both halves and solve; do not guess and check.
Check the answer is possible: you cannot pay down more payables than you have cash or than you owe.
A target achieved by paying down payables is a target achieved without improving the business.
