7.5  Using the method honestly
Everything in this chapter can be used to improve a ratio without improving the business, and both the exam and real lenders know it.
Paying down payables just before the reporting date raises the current ratio of any company whose ratio is above 1, and leaves working capital untouched. Delaying a purchase does the same. Classifying a borrowing as noncurrent when a waiver was obtained after the reporting date is the Part 1 version of the same manoeuvre.
The defences are three. Look at several dates, because a ratio that moves and reverts is suspect. Look at the cash flow ratio, which is a flow over the whole year and cannot be arranged on one day. And look at working capital in dollars beside the ratio, because the manoeuvres that move the ratio usually leave working capital exactly where it was — which is the clearest possible signal that nothing happened.
Figure F507-06 pairs each manoeuvre with its tell.
Figure F507-06. Improving a ratio without improving the business
@table
Manoeuvre | What it does to the current ratio | What it does to working capital | The tell
Pay down payables at the year end | Raises it | Nothing | Working capital unchanged; payables jump back in January
Delay a purchase until January | Raises it | Nothing | Inventory and payables both low at one date only
Draw less of a credit line at the year end | Raises it | Nothing | The facility note
Sell receivables to a factor | Raises the cash ratio | Reduces it by the fee | A loss on sale of receivables in the income statement
@end
The last row is one Orontes actually did: its income statement carries a loss on sale of receivables of $15,000, which is the fee paid to Gulf Factor Co. The transaction turned receivables into cash, improved the cash ratio, and cost $15,000. Whether that was worth doing is a working-capital question, and Book 2 answers it.
