Chapter summary
Figure F507-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F507-07. Chapter 7 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Adding the same amount to both halves of a ratio moves it towards 1.0, and subtracting the same amount from both moves it away: so buying inventory on credit lowers Orontes's current ratio of 3.31 and paying a supplier raises it, while a company below 1.0 sees the reverse. Transactions touching one half only move the ratio in the obvious direction, and three catch candidates out: a fully provided write-off changes nothing, a declared dividend lowers every ratio before any cash moves, and collecting a receivable moves only the cash ratio. Unit sales work through the statements rather than onto one line, and a growing company can become less liquid because it pays its suppliers before its customers pay it — Orontes's Gulf order takes the current ratio to 2.95 before recovering to 3.41. Impact is computed by restating both halves and dividing, and a target ratio is reached by solving an equation with the unknown in both halves. Finally, every method here can raise a ratio without improving the business; the tell is working capital standing still while the ratio moves, and the cash flow ratio is the one that cannot be arranged on a single day.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P7-01  A company's current ratio is 1.8. It buys inventory on account. The current ratio will:
A.	rise.
B.	fall.
C.	not change.
D.	fall only if the purchase exceeds working capital.
