C.	it excludes inventory.
D.	it is audited separately.
Case-style practice: Barada before the review
Barada Wholesale's year end is 31 December and the bank reviews its credit line in February. Barada's current ratio at 31 December was 2.00; at 30 November it had been 1.62. During December Barada paid $420,000 of accounts payable using its overdraft, which the bank classifies as a current liability, and delayed a $300,000 stock purchase until January.
Required. Say what each of the two December actions does to the current ratio, and whether the move from 1.62 to 2.00 represents an improvement in Barada's position. Name the two figures you would ask for to settle it. Continuous prose, under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC7-1  Answer B. Adding the same amount to both halves of a ratio above 1 moves it towards 1.
A is wrong. A rise would require the numerator to grow proportionately more.
C is wrong. Both halves grow by the same amount, so the ratio changes.
D is wrong. The direction does not depend on the amount, only the starting ratio.
SC7-2  Answer B. Subtracting the same amount from both halves of a ratio below 1 moves it away from 1, so it falls.
A is wrong. That is the result for a ratio above 1.
C is wrong. Both halves change, so the ratio changes.
D is wrong. A ratio of assets to liabilities cannot go negative here.
SC7-3  Answer C. Both halves fall by the same amount, so the difference is unchanged.
A is wrong. Nothing was added to current assets.
B is wrong. The liability fell by the same amount as the asset.
D is wrong. Working capital is a difference and does not depend on the ratio.
SC7-4  Answer C. The allowance already reduced the net receivable, so the net figure does not move.
A is wrong. Neither half changes.
B is wrong. Neither half changes.
D is wrong. The allowance is reduced by the same amount as the gross receivable.
SC7-5  Answer B. A current liability appears and nothing is added to current assets.
A is wrong. The denominator grows.
C is wrong. The liability arises on declaration, not on payment.
D is wrong. Declaration creates the obligation.
SC7-6  Answer C. Cash and securities rise while current assets and quick assets are unchanged in total.
A is wrong. Current assets change form, not amount.
B is wrong. Both cash and receivables are quick assets.
D is wrong. The cash ratio does change.
SC7-7  Answer B. Quick assets gain the receivable of 100 and lose nothing, while current assets gain only the profit of 40.
A is wrong. Both ratios share the same denominator.
C is wrong. Inventory is excluded from quick assets.
D is wrong. The credit term is why cash does not move, not why quick rises more.
SC7-8  Answer B. Cash leaves and inventory arrives, so the current ratio holds and the quick ratio falls.
A is wrong. Total current assets are unchanged.
C is wrong. Cash is falling.
D is wrong. Working capital is unchanged.
SC7-9  Answer B. The cash goes out to the supplier before it comes in from the customer.
A is wrong. Tax reduces profit, not liquidity timing.
C is wrong. There is no such rule.
D is wrong. Inventory is a current asset.
SC7-10  Answer C. The quick numerator gains nothing while the denominator grows.
A is wrong. The denominator grows, so the ratio falls.
B is wrong. The effect is larger, not smaller.
D is wrong. Both ratios fall.
SC7-11  Answer A. (14,188,100 − x) ÷ (4,280,000 − x) = 4.00 gives 3x = 2,931,900 and x = 977,300.
B is wrong. This does not satisfy the equation.
C is wrong. This is 3x, not x.
D is wrong. This is the 2024 current liabilities figure.
SC7-12  Answer B. The manoeuvres move the ratio and leave the difference exactly where it was.
A is wrong. A rise can be genuine.
C is wrong. Profitability is a separate question.
D is wrong. The transactions are properly recorded; that is the point.
SC7-13  Answer D. Its numerator is a year of operating cash flow and cannot be arranged on one day.
A is wrong. The current ratio is the easiest to move.
B is wrong. The quick ratio moves with a payables payment.
C is wrong. The cash ratio moves with any cash transaction.
