Item | Answer
Buy $720,000 of inventory on credit | 14,908,100 and 5,000,000, giving 2.98.
Issue shares for $1,000,000 cash | 15,188,100 and 4,280,000, giving 3.55.
Buy equipment for $1,180,000 cash | 13,008,100 and 4,280,000, giving 3.04.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P7-01  Answer B. Both halves rise by the same amount and the ratio is above 1, so it falls towards 1.
A is wrong. A rise needs the numerator to grow proportionately more.
C is wrong. The ratio changes whenever the halves change unequally in proportion.
D is wrong. The direction does not depend on the size.
P7-02  Answer B. Subtracting from both halves of a ratio below 1 moves it away from 1.
A is wrong. That is the case above 1.
C is wrong. The ratio changes.
D is wrong. It moves away from 1, not to it.
P7-03  Answer B. Collecting a receivable moves cash within current assets.
A is wrong. Share issue raises current assets only.
C is wrong. Paying a payable changes both halves.
D is wrong. A declared dividend raises current liabilities.
P7-04  Answer A. 13,688,100 ÷ 3,780,000 = 3.62.
B is wrong. This is not the result of the arithmetic.
C is wrong. The ratio changed.
D is wrong. 2.95 is the Gulf-order figure from the worked example.
P7-05  Answer C. The allowance already reduced the net receivable.
A is wrong. Nothing moves.
B is wrong. Nothing moves.
D is wrong. Gross receivables and the allowance fall equally.
P7-06  Answer C. Current assets rise by the profit: receivables up 100, inventory down 60.
A is wrong. This ignores the inventory given up.
B is wrong. This is the cost, not the change.
D is wrong. A profitable sale adds the profit to working capital.
P7-07  Answer A. Cash becomes inventory, so the total is unchanged and the quick numerator falls.
B is wrong. The current total is unchanged.
C is wrong. The quick ratio falls.
D is wrong. The quick ratio changes.
P7-08  Answer B. 3x = 17,120,000 − 14,188,100 = 2,931,900, so x = 977,300.
A is wrong. This does not satisfy the equation.
C is wrong. This does not satisfy the equation.
D is wrong. This is 3x.
P7-09  Answer B. Factoring converts receivables to cash and records a loss equal to the fee.
A is wrong. A share issue does not touch income.
C is wrong. Buying inventory for cash lowers the cash ratio.
D is wrong. A dividend is not an expense.
P7-10  Answer B. A year of operating cash flow cannot be rearranged by transactions in the last week.
A is wrong. The cash flow ratio uses current liabilities.
C is wrong. Excluding inventory describes the quick ratio.
D is wrong. There is no separate audit of a ratio.
Case-style set: Barada before the review
Each December action raises the ratio and neither improves the position.
Paying $420,000 of accounts payable using the overdraft substitutes one current liability for another, so current liabilities are unchanged and current assets are unchanged: on these facts alone the current ratio does not move at all. If instead the bank had treated the overdraft as outside current liabilities, the ratio would have risen with no change whatever in what Barada owes. Which treatment applies is the first thing to settle.
Delaying the $300,000 purchase until January keeps both inventory and payables off the 31 December balance sheet. For a ratio above 1, removing an equal amount from both halves raises it. Working capital is unchanged.
So the move from 1.62 to 2.00 is not an improvement in Barada's position. The company owes the same, holds the same, and has simply reported on a day chosen to flatter. The reversal will be visible in January, when the purchase is made and the payable reappears.
Two figures would settle it. The first is Barada's current ratio at 31 January, which shows whether the December figure reverts. The second is its cash flow ratio for the year — operating cash flow over current liabilities — because a flow across twelve months cannot be arranged in the last week of one of them.
